### A4 — Live tenant 54 evidence

Admin `charlienacario884`:

```text
POST /cms-akira-shell/permissions       -> 303 /cms-akira-shell/permissions?saved=1
POST /cms-akira-shell/users/2/role      -> 303 /cms-akira-shell/users?saved=1
POST /cms-akira-shell/users/2/active    -> 303 /cms-akira-shell/users?saved=1
```

The permission proof retained the same role set and created active policy version 2. User role and activity remained `author` and active; the two successful mutations incremented `token_version` from 0 to 2.

Disallowed caller `akiraauthor`, role `author`, with valid CSRF:

```text
POST /cms-akira-shell/permissions       -> 403 role_not_allowed
POST /cms-akira-shell/users/2/role      -> 403 role_not_allowed
POST /cms-akira-shell/users/2/active    -> 403 role_not_allowed
```

Each response named its required capability. All three produced `route.authority.denied` records with:

```text
state=declared
caller_module=cms-akira-shell
actor_role=author
reason=role_not_allowed
```

Before/after denial snapshot was identical:

```json
{
  "max_policy": 2,
  "policy": {
    "policy_version": 2,
    "allowed_roles": "admin,administrator,superadmin",
    "grant_state": "granted",
    "is_active": 1
  },
  "user": {
    "id": 2,
    "role": "author",
    "is_active": 1,
    "token_version": 2
  }
}
```

Thus the denied handler bodies did not mutate policy or user data.

